++# Agent Note: Plugin-contributed composer key handlers
++
++Status: proposed
++
++English | [中文](2026-10-11-client-composer-key-handlers.zh.md)
++
++## Problem
++
++The composer's keyboard behavior is unreachable from outside `ui-conversation`. `registerComposerKeymap` binds the editor's command layer, but it is package-private, its handlers close over bar state the package owns, and no service or slot exposes a gesture seam. A client plugin that needs a composer keyboard gesture — prompt-history recall, snippet expansion, vim-style motion — has three options, none of them an extension point:
++
++- Scrape the DOM and write the draft through `document.execCommand`/synthetic events, which bypasses the editor's transaction, undo history, and reference-chip spans.
++- Read the draft from `InputState.draft` and write it through `inputActions.setDraft`, but still locate the composer element and compute the caret from the DOM: there is no public caret read (`ComposerKeyboard.caretSpan()` is documented as package-internal, never across a plugin boundary) and the card's internal attributes are not a contract.
++- Attach a document-level listener, which cannot order itself against the editor's own handling and silently fights any other plugin doing the same.
++
++Because neither `conversation.composer.dock` (Session-scoped, suppressed in Hero mode) nor `conversation.input.dock` (Session-scoped list) can carry an apply-level listener for a plugin whose gesture must also work while composing a blank Session, a plugin has no correct place to put the listener at all.
++
++The narrow form of the gap is recorded in [discussion #3981](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/discussions/3981) (a stable DOM marker or a locator API for the composer) and its Hero-mode sibling [#6118](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/discussions/6118). Both accept the DOM as the fallback surface; this proposal removes the need for it.
++
++## Proposal
++
++Add a root-scope `ComposerKeys` registry to the `conversation` service (`ctx.conversation.composerKeys`):
++
++- `add(handler): () => void` registers one `ComposerKeyHandler` — a stable `id`, an optional ascending `order`, and `handle(context): 'handled' | 'pass'`.
++- `handlers(): readonly ComposerKeyHandler[]` is the composer-side read, sorted by `order` then registration order (a stable sort over an insertion-ordered array).
++- `ComposerKeyContext` carries the live facts a handler needs without DOM access: the bubbling `KeyboardEvent`, `sessionId`, `InputState.draft`, the detect projection the caret indexes into (`caretDraft`), `caret` as an `EditSelection`, the IME-composition flag, `editable`, and the public `InputActions` face.
++
++The composer bar consults the registry from one bubbling `keydown` listener on `[data-composer-card]`. Ordering is the whole design: the editor's root listener runs first (target phase), so a gesture the keymap, an open trigger menu, or an IME guard consumed arrives with `defaultPrevented` set and is skipped; the bar's own keymap keeps `COMMAND_PRIORITY_CRITICAL`. Handlers are consulted in registry order and the loop stops at the first `'handled'`, which the listener answers with `preventDefault()`. Because the bar is the resident `session-maybe` entry, a handler registered from a plugin's `apply` covers the composer in every state it is on screen, the blank-Session Hero included.
++
++The registry is additive: an empty registry short-circuits before building any context, and no existing gesture path changes.
++
++## Alternatives considered
++
++**Publish the caret in `InputState`.** A reactive caret re-renders every consumer on every cursor move, and the package deliberately keeps it an event-handler read (`caretSpan()`); the registry hands the same value to exactly the code that acts on a key.
++
++**Add `SessionInputResolver.forSession(id)` plus a public `caret()` read.** This lets a plugin resolve the facade from a root-scope listener, but the plugin still has to locate the composer element, decide editability from internal DOM attributes, and re-derive the composer's own consumption (menu arbitration, IME guard) from `defaultPrevented` after the fact. It also adds an id-addressed path next to the scope-addressed one for the same object.
++
++**Register handlers inside Lexical's command layer.** `registerComposerKeymap` would have to expose its `ComposerKeymapHandlers`, which are bar-owned closures over submit/steer/paste policy; contributing to them from a plugin would put plugin code inside the bar's decision order rather than after it. The DOM-boundary listener keeps plugin gestures outside the composer's own policy.
++
++**A keybinding string table (`"ArrowUp": handler`).** The gesture vocabulary is the browser's, not the harness's; matching stays with the handler, and the registry carries no key parsing or normalization policy.
++
++## Acceptance criteria
++
++- Registry tests prove ascending-order consultation, registration-order tie-breaking, stop-at-first-`'handled'`, idempotent disposal, and the empty-registry answer.
++- Composer tests prove a registered handler is consulted after the editor, receives the live draft/caret/composing/editable/actions facts, consumes a `'handled'` gesture, is skipped when an earlier consumer already prevented the gesture, and that no handler means no behavior change.
++- A chip in the draft proves `caretDraft` carries the projection `caret` indexes into: the clipboard `draft` carries the chip's expansion while `caretDraft` carries its one-character detect form.
++- A handler registered with `ctx.effect` unregisters with its fiber; the HMR-safety path observes the removal.
++- `ComposerKeys` is exported from the package's `./client` entry together with `EditSelection`, so a plugin can type its handler without importing package internals.
++- The change adds no model-visible input, no session event, and no Host surface: token cost and KV-cache behavior are unchanged.
++
++## Risks
++
++- A plugin handler that returns `'handled'` for a gesture the composer needs (Enter, Space, Escape) can break submission. Consultation happens after the composer's own consumers, so only unclaimed gestures are reachable; documentation names the remaining obligation to key off the event.
++- The `editable` fact is computed by the bar; a handler that writes the draft still has to tolerate a session switching between the read and the write. `InputActions.setDraft` is Session-scoped and resolves at call time, so a late write lands on the addressed Session, not the focused composer.
++- A near-identical ask ([#3981](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/discussions/3981)) accepts a DOM-marker solution instead. If the maintainers prefer the marker, this registry remains the smaller plugin-visible surface: it removes the plugin's need to read or write the DOM at all.
